<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The app's primary App Store category, declared once for both
	     destinations. It is upload validation that requires it for a *Mac App
	     Store* submission; the iOS listing takes its category from App Store
	     Connect, so there this only states the same fact in the bundle. Kept
	     even though today's macOS channel is Developer ID + notarization rather
	     than the Mac App Store (see docs/RELEASING.md, "Not here yet"), so
	     switching channels later does not fail validation on a key nobody
	     remembered. -->
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.developer-tools</string>
	<!-- Export-compliance answer, pre-answered so every upload skips the
	     interactive question. `false` is the standard exemption: the app's only
	     cryptography is HTTPS/TLS, and all of it comes from Apple's own
	     frameworks (URL loading, the Security framework Keychain) or libgit2's
	     Apple TLS backend — no proprietary or non-exempt encryption is
	     implemented or bundled. Must be a real Boolean, not the string "NO";
	     `ReleaseMetadataTests` asserts the type. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!-- ==== Sparkle 2 (macOS auto-update) ====================================
	     The two keys Sparkle reads out of the main bundle. Neither has an
	     `INFOPLIST_KEY_*` equivalent, so `GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE` cannot
	     produce them and they belong here rather than in project.yml.

	     Both keys land in the *iOS* bundle too — there is one partial plist and
	     it is merged into both destinations' generated plists — where nothing
	     reads them: Sparkle links on macOS only (`destinationFilters: [macOS]`)
	     and every line of `SoftwareUpdater.swift` is inside `#if os(macOS)`.
	     That is the same deliberate consequence
	     `INFOPLIST_KEY_LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace` documents in reverse
	     (an iOS-only key that is a no-op on macOS); two inert strings in the iOS
	     bundle are cheaper than splitting the plist in two.

	     Deliberately absent: `SUEnableAutomaticChecks` and
	     `SUScheduledCheckInterval`. With neither key present Sparkle asks the
	     user once, on first launch, whether it may check automatically — which
	     is exactly the wanted UX, and it costs no machinery of ours. Adding
	     either key would decide that question on the user's behalf. -->
	<!-- The update feed. `releases/latest/download/<asset>` is GitHub's stable
	     redirect to the newest release's asset of that name: every release the
	     workflow publishes carries its own `appcast.xml`, and this URL always
	     resolves to the current one. A per-release URL
	     (`releases/download/v1.0/appcast.xml`) would be baked into the shipped
	     binary and go stale the moment v1.1 exists, so an installed v1.0 would
	     keep reading v1.0's feed and never see another update. The asset name in
	     `.github/workflows/release.yml` must equal this URL's last path
	     component; `ReleaseWorkflowTests` asserts that cross-file pair. -->
	<key>SUFeedURL</key>
	<string>https://github.com/HawkeyePierce89/pisaka/releases/latest/download/appcast.xml</string>
	<!-- The public half of the EdDSA (ed25519) key pair Sparkle verifies every
	     downloaded update against. This is the REAL key, generated 2026-08-16
	     with `bin/generate_keys` from the pinned Sparkle 2.9.5 tarball
	     (procedure in docs/RELEASING.md). The private half lives only in the
	     `SPARKLE_PRIVATE_EDDSA_KEY` GitHub repository secret and in an offline
	     backup — never in this repository. The release workflow still greps this
	     file for the retired placeholder value and refuses to run if it ever
	     reappears (a revert of this key would ship a release signed by a key no
	     installed copy trusts).

	     Changing or losing the private half is unrecoverable for copies already
	     installed: they verify against the string below, baked into their own
	     bundle, and will reject every update signed by any other key — the only
	     way out is each user downloading a new build by hand. -->
	<key>SUPublicEDKey</key>
	<string>IgLR74DklU05BloO6JbnXcm4cwUaCLUABRDpP5ZZcYQ=</string>
</dict>
</plist>
